+#### Keyed nonce authorization and state growth
+
+Validation for a non-zero key set must authenticate at least `(sender, nonce_keys_hash, nonce_seq)`. Checking only `nonce_seq`, or only the first key when multiple keys are selected, lets an attacker consume unauthorized replay domains. Disjoint keys remove replay ordering but do not isolate sender balance, payer balance, account storage, or other shared validation dependencies.
+
+Every first-use non-zero key creates one persistent `NONCE_MANAGER` slot. The state-gas charge bounds this growth through explicit frame and block state-gas limits. A keyed transaction does not advance the sender's legacy account nonce; applications whose `CREATE` address depends on that nonce must authenticate `TXPARAM(0x0D)` or use `CREATE2`.
+
+#### Nonceless replay capacity and identity
+
+Nonceless safety depends on consensus-enforced expiry and replay state, not on best-effort mempool deduplication. The pre-execution live-map and current-ring checks make same-block duplicates and premature ring reuse invalid. A full live ring safely rejects new nonceless transactions, but under-sized activation parameters would still create an availability failure; L1 values therefore remain an explicit Draft gate until benchmarked against the maximum accepted throughput.
+
+The replay ID deliberately ignores fees, frame gas limits, and raw signature bytes. Every replacement must nevertheless validate independently under the canonical signature hash, which does commit to its actual limits, fees, frames, and signature metadata. Implementations must never substitute the full transaction hash for the logical replay ID.
+
+#### Signer-binding authority
+
+Returning a digest from a signer-binding frame is equivalent to authorizing `ECRECOVER(digest, ...) == frame.target` for the rest of the transaction. Account implementations must verify the application digest, not merely the canonical transaction hash, and must bind it to the intended action. Conflicting bindings revert, and `MAX_BOUND_SIGNERS` bounds transaction-scoped table growth. The table is never persisted across transactions.
+
 #### Arbitrary Signature Malleability
 
 The protocol performs no cryptographic validation for `ARBITRARY` signatures. When an `ARBITRARY` signature has empty `msg`, its raw bytes are elided from the canonical signature hash and can therefore be changed without changing `TXPARAM(0x08)`. If the custom verifier accepts multiple encodings for the same witness the transaction hash may be malleable even though the canonical signature hash is not. Custom verifiers should enforce canonical encodings and reject unused bytes. When an `ARBITRARY` signature uses an explicit 32-byte `msg`, its raw bytes are committed to by the canonical signature hash and this particular source of transaction-hash malleability does not apply.
